WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.179 --> 00:00:04.680
Welcome to the Science Asylum.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.640
I am Nick Lucid.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.030
So I was watching Star Wars recently and noticed
Han Solo said this:

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:13.160
“It’s the ship that made the Kessel Run
in less than 12 parsecs.”

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:17.119
Actually, a “parsec” is a unit of distance,
so that claim makes no physical sense.

00:00:17.119 --> 00:00:20.340
Hmmm, we’re going to try to make some sense
of it anyway.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:24.300
For those you who don’t know, the Kessel
Run is an 18 parsec strip of space in the

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.160
Star Wars galaxy that smugglers like Han Solo
use to transport spice.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.820
It’s kinda tough to get through because it’s
monitored by imperial forces.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:34.470
Not that imperial forces are known for being
good shots or even all that smart.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:38.390
Anyway, let’s start with a little dose of
reality about writing stories.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:41.649
In the revised 4th draft of the script of
Star Wars: A New Hope

00:00:41.649 --> 00:00:44.430
Immediately following that line from Han,
it says:

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:48.600
“Ben reacts to Solo's stupid attempt to
impress them with obvious misinformation.”

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.180
which seems to suggest Han was purposefully
giving them a load of crap.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:52.900
However!

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.840
In the official audio commentary, George Lucas
claims:

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.560
Han used units of parsecs because the Millennium
Falcon’s navigation system is so sophisticated,

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.680
that it can calculate a straighter path through
hyperspace.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.960
Which seems to suggest Han was telling the
truth.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:07.280
Back Peddle, Back Peddle, Back Peddle

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.080
See, totally inconsistent.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:10.460
Yes, it's inconsistent

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:14.560
but we like to think of books and movies
as though they contain completely self-consistent

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.460
worlds that actually exist.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:20.020
So what would it take to actually shorten
18 parsecs to 12?

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:23.750
First, if we’re going to use a unit like
“parsec” then we better know what it means.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:27.049
It’s actually short for “Parallax of an
arc-second”.

00:01:27.049 --> 00:01:29.620
To make some sense of it, we’re going to
have to zoom out.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:31.580
No no, like way out.

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:34.420
Ugh just keep going until I tell you to stop.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:36.620
Keep going, Keep going.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:37.560
OK, stop!

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:40.450
Out here, you can see the entire orbit of
the Earth.

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:43.770
If we look at this near-by star, it looks
like it’s by these distance stars.

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:46.050
6 months later though, it looks like it’s
over here.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:49.270
It’s kind of like when you’re driving
down the road and the far away things look

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:51.979
like they’re moving a lot slower than the
closer things.

00:01:51.979 --> 00:01:55.430
That difference is called “parallax,”
which is the same thing your two eyes use

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:56.430
to give you depth perception.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.660
Camera one, Camera two, Camera one, Camera
two.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:01.080
An “arc-second” is an angle measurement.

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:06.120
1/3600th of a degree to be precise
Just like how a second is 1/3600th of an hour.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.000
So, like, a super small angle!

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.620
Divisions like this are left over from Ancient
Babylon, where they…

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:11.640
HEY!!!

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:20.639
If the Earth’s parallax of a near-by star
forms an angle of one arc-second,

00:02:20.639 --> 00:02:23.669
then the star is said to be one parsec away
from Earth,

00:02:23.669 --> 00:02:27.460
which is about three and a quarter light years
or 19 trillion miles .

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:30.560
He couldn’t have used the unit anyway because
Earth doesn’t exist in the

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:31.600
Star Wars galaxy.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:36.480
Maybe not, but human’s evolved on Earth partly
because the Sun is just the right size and

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.640
Earth is at just the right distance away.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:42.599
Any fictional planet that evolves humans would
have a comparably-sized orbit.

00:02:42.599 --> 00:02:43.599
Boom!

00:02:43.599 --> 00:02:46.879
So the question remains: How do we turn 18
parsecs into 12?

00:02:46.879 --> 00:02:50.010
The ships in Star Wars jump into something
called “hyperspace.”

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:52.489
Actually, hyperspace is completely fictional.

00:02:52.489 --> 00:02:54.340
It doesn’t matter if it’s fictional.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.340
Just go with me here.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:59.519
We can assume the fictional physics of hyperspace
doesn’t shortened distances because, if

00:02:59.519 --> 00:03:02.090
that were true, Han wouldn’t be boasting
about distance.

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:03.309
It doesn’t make any sense.

00:03:03.309 --> 00:03:07.819
We can also assume hyperspace isn’t just
slang for “almost light speed” because,

00:03:07.819 --> 00:03:11.859
at nearly light speed,
even 12 parsecs would take about 40 years.

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:14.000
The delivery dates would be totally impractical.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.620
We also know everything with mass travels
slower than the speed of light,

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:21.700
which leaves us with only one option for hyperspace:
It’s a portion of space where the speed

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:26.820
of light is faster than usual so, inside hyperspace,
you can travel much faster than usual.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:30.880
And the navigation computer just has a map
of where it all is and which parts are safe.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:34.320
Since the speed of light is faster, you’re
still not moving at a significant fraction

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.680
of it, so special relativity doesn’t apply, but

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:38.579
general relativity still can!

00:03:38.579 --> 00:03:43.549
As we’ve seen before, gravity affects objects
by changing what straight lines look like.

00:03:43.549 --> 00:03:47.049
Changing the shape of space is also going
to change its size, but a small star like

00:03:47.049 --> 00:03:48.439
the Sun isn’t going to cut it.

00:03:48.439 --> 00:03:50.430
We’re going to need a bigger mass.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:54.839
Star Wars fans have suggested Han used a set
of black holes called the Maw Cluster.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:56.560
Actually, that’s not Star Wars canon.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:59.400
That’s Expanded Universe, which makes it Star Wars Legends.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:00.560
Didn’t you get that memo?

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:01.370
Fine!

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:04.120
18 parsecs is a pretty big region of space.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:07.480
We can assume somewhere there’s at least
one black hole.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.140
Completely empty space is described by this
equation.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:13.739
The space around a black hole is described
by this equation.

00:04:13.739 --> 00:04:18.200
The identical stuff shows how space is unaffected
by the black hole, so we’ll ignore those.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:22.240
We’re also only interested in space, not
time, so we’ll ignore this too.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:26.639
And we can see now, the black hole only affects
space along radial lines.

00:04:26.639 --> 00:04:27.990
If we take a ruler and…

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:28.990
Wait a second!

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:31.520
The distances are bigger when you’re closer
to the black hole!

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.180
Getting close to black holes makes the Kessel
Run longer, not shorter.

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:36.620
Ha! I win!

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:39.320
So, have you ever thought this deeply about
a fictional world?

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:40.440
If so, which one?!

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:41.460
Let us know in the comments.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.760
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to
be a little crazy.

